  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Evaluating Artworks\' Locations**\n\nI\'ve just observed the exact file paths for the five generated artworks, noting their location within the `/src/assets/images/` directory. These are: `art_alpine_twilight_1795278906644.jpg`, `art_botanical_atelier_1795278908731.jpg`, `art_`, and the locations of the remaining two pieces.\n\n\n**Analyzing Image Placement**\n\nI\'ve finalized the file paths for all five artworks: `/src/assets/images/art_brutalist_courtyard_1795278907758.jpg`, `/src/assets/images/art_coastal_horizon_1795278908179.jpg`, and `/src/assets/images/art_abstract_ochre_1795278909230.jpg`. I\'m also considering how to best handle the texture mapping in the 3D canvas, and I\'ve come up with a solution for images with canvas edges. I\'m focusing on ensuring these images fit within frames cleanly. Now, I\'m shifting focus to Firebase setup and designing the Firestore data model with secure rules in mind.\n\n\n**Defining Museum Data Structure**\n\nMy focus is now on structuring the museum data within Firestore. I\'m currently defining the fields for the master museum document, including details like owner ID, title, description, and access code, with constraints on string lengths and data types. I am also working on `memberEmails` and verifying them against Pillar 1 and Pillar 8, and the data will be securely handled using Firestore rules. I\'m also considering storing a member subcollection as recommended.\n\n\n**Refining Member Permissions**\n\nI\'m now refining member permissions. My current approach involves a strict `memberEmails` array (maximum 10 entries) within each `/museums/{museumId}` document to store email addresses for secure access. I\'m focusing on \"Total Array Guarding\" for validation and adhering to Pillar 8 principles. I am exploring the use of an access code lookup document (`/museumCodes/{code}`) that helps potential members obtain the `museumId`. I\'m also ensuring the code provides no Personally Identifiable Information (PII). I\'m also determining how new members join and gain access.\n\n\n**Clarifying User Access Methods**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the two key user access methods. I\'m prioritizing email-based access, ensuring it\'s the primary permission mechanism for airtight security. This will be granted with a Firebase rule. Secondly, I\'m integrating access codes, which will be stored in `/museumCodes/{code}`, to securely resolve `museumId` without PII. I am designing a seamless system where both the methods can be used together. I\'m focusing on open access via codes or specific invitations. I\'m making it possible for any signed-in user who knows the `museumId` to get `/museums/{museumId}` if `isOpenByCode: boolean` is toggled on by the owner.\n\n\n**Structuring Artwork Storage**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the `/museums/{museumId}/artworks/{artworkId}` collection. I\'m carefully defining its fields, including `museumId`, `ownerId`, and other artwork metadata, such as `title`, `artist`, `imageUrl` and `frameStyle`. I\'m especially focused on implementing airtight security rules and eliminating any blanket `list` reads. I\'m also ensuring the data structure is optimized for efficient queries and data integrity.\n\n\n**Defining Member Data Storage**\n\nI\'m now determining data storage for member information. I plan to use the `/museums/{museumId}/members/{memberId}` structure, including fields like `museumId`, `email`, `displayName`, and `role`. I\'ll implement security rules so only the owner can manage members, and members can access their own entry. I\'m also planning to capture real-time visitor presence via `/museums/{museumId}/presence/{visitorId}`.\n\n\n**Validating Museum Schema**\n\nI\'m now deeply engaged in validating the new schema against all eight security pillars. I\'m focusing on rigorously enforcing access control for `/museums/{museumId}/presence/{visitorId}` documents, ensuring that visitors can exclusively `create`, `update`, and `delete` their own presence data based on their user ID (`uid`). I\'m also confirming the rule that users can only `list` if they have `ownerId`, `uid`, `isOpenByCode` access. I\'m double-checking the use of `get()` on parent `/museums/{museumId}` for writes and single-doc gets.\n\n\n**Validating Validation Logic**\n\nI\'m now diving into the validation blueprints. I\'m focusing on ensuring robust validation for all entities. I\'m meticulously implementing `isValidUser`, `isValidMuseum`, and all the others. I\'m emphasizing `hasAll`/`hasOnly` for creates and `affectedKeys().hasOnly(...)` for updates, with rigorous type safety and size bounds on every string and list to ensure everything adheres to all eight security pillars.\n\n\n**Finalizing Rule Architecture**\n\nI\'m now putting the finishing touches on the Firebase security rules. I\'m focusing on ensuring all `list` operations include explicit checks against user IDs, verified emails, and the `isOpenByCode` flag, adhering to Pillar 8 principles. I am reviewing the strict isolation for PII across `/users/{userId}` and member data. My objective is to ensure atomicity and consistency via `exists()` checks and that all fields are immutable where required. Also, I am finalizing the default-deny rules, and other security requirements.\n\n\n"
    message_id: "-cgBa4bmIdGX_uMPuay5iQs"
