  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Verifying Deployment Safety**\n\nI\'m pleased to report that ESLint found no errors in `DRAFT_firestore.rules`. However, I\'ve decided to proceed cautiously. Before promoting it to `firestore.rules` and deploying, I\'m initiating a thorough **Phase 5 Red Team Audit**. I intend to meticulously evaluate the ruleset against every conceivable adversarial attack scenario. My process is currently: 1. **[begins]**\n\n\n**Analyzing Red Team Concerns**\n\nI\'ve made great strides in the audit. The Red Team\'s concerns about potential logic leaks in `canCurateMuseum(museumId)` function are now the focus. I\'m scrutinizing `canCurateMuseum()` within the context of `artworks` `create` and `update` rules, to see if anything is accessible to unauthorized individuals.\n\n\n**Refining Access Control Logic**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the access control logic, specifically the differentiation between curators and visitors. My current focus involves assessing the `memberEmails` field within the existing `canCurateMuseum()` function and refining the implementation. I believe implementing a bounded `curatorEmails` list would ensure the necessary access restrictions, permitting curators to upload artwork while keeping visitors read-only. I\'m planning to introduce the `curatorEmails` field within the `Museum` definition and update the rules accordingly to better enforce Tier 2 user type functionality.\n\n\n**Finalizing Batch Transactions**\n\nI\'m now integrating crucial checks for museum code creation. To handle atomic batch operations correctly, I\'m verifying the existence of the museum using `existsAfter(...)` when creating the museum and its code in the same batch. My goal is for the rule to permit the museum creation with museum code at the same time within a single write batch. With this update, batch creation should be seamless. I\'ll update `firebase-blueprint.json` and prepare for final deployment soon!\n\n\n"
