  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Deploying Security Rules**\n\nI successfully deployed the Firebase security rules after they passed ESLint checks. Now, I\'m shifting focus to modularizing the application\'s architecture. I\'m starting by listing the modules under `src/`, beginning with the stylesheet.\n\n\n**Defining Museum Interfaces**\n\nI\'ve crafted complete TypeScript domain interfaces for the museum, including crucial elements such as gender, avatar configuration, artwork items, and member details. Furthermore, curated presets for hair styles, outfits, and skin tones are integrated. Now, I\'m turning my attention to the museum\'s layout.\n\n\n**Visualizing Gallery Expansion**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how the gallery space expands dynamically. I\'m exploring the logic for adding new wings based on artwork count, using calculations for bay counts. Currently, I\'m imagining a system where each wing holds two artworks, one on each facing wall, at 7.5m intervals. I\'m calculating bay counts based on curated seeds and considering user interaction with an \"Add Exhibition Photo\" button.\n\n\n**Calculating Gallery Expansion**\n\nI\'ve refined the gallery expansion logic. Adding the seventh image instantly extends the museum to Bay 4, and the ninth to Bay 5. This two-artwork-per-bay system creates a visually rewarding growth effect. Now, I\'m defining two large curated wall bays per gallery section and an end pavilion. The `getArtworkPlacement` function now returns detailed placement data, and I\'m developing `clampPlayerPosition` to constrain user movement within the gallery.\n\n\n**Refining User Experience**\n\nI\'m now implementing collision detection to prevent the user from clipping through walls and furniture. My focus is on ensuring a smooth, intuitive navigation experience. I\'ve also incorporated chronological sorting and grouping of artworks by date, considering both `dateCaptured` and a tie-breaker. Extensive unit tests now validate the museum\'s layout, placement calculations, collision clamping, and input validation.\n\n**Building Data Structures**\n\nI\'ve built the structure for a curated initial exhibition with a sample of artworks. It will use the generated artworks and avatar presets to make the user experience immediate. I\'m also ensuring Firebase initialization with a test connection, and have defined key operations for the museum service.\n\n\n**Implementing Database Integration**\n\nI\'ve integrated Google Sign-In with Firestore CRUD operations, including real-time listeners for critical data such as user profiles, museum data (owned, shared, and join-by-code), artwork, members, and visitor presence. I\'ve also incorporated local persistence for Guest Preview mode.\n\nNext, I have focused on procedural textures. I\'ve created procedural HTML5 Canvas PBR textures, which include warm travertine, Venetian plaster, walnut wood grain, radial soft contact shadows, accession plaque textures for artwork titles, and floating visitor nameplates.\nI\'m now building a high-craft composite procedural 3D human.\n\n\n**Constructing Avatar Details**\n\nMy focus is now on the avatar builder\'s detailed anatomy and wardrobe. I\'m fleshing out distinct male and female proportions, including tailored clothing and sculpted hair. I\'m ensuring smooth articulation for natural movement, particularly in the arms and legs, and designing accessories to match. I\'m also ensuring a consistent high-craft aesthetic across all elements.\n\n\n**Designing Museum Details**\n\nI\'m now detailing the museum\'s 3D architecture, starting with the entrance rotunda and foyer, complete with classical columns and a welcome inscription. I\'m building dynamically expandable gallery wings with marble floors, walnut baseboards, and themed walls. Each wing will feature fluted pilasters, coffered ceilings with skylights, and track lighting for artworks. I\'m also adding a tufted leather bench and planning a gallery pass lanyard for avatars.\n\n\n**Adding Museum Aesthetics**\n\nI\'m now focusing on museum aesthetics. I\'m adding velvet benches with bronze legs every second wing, plus a Far North End Pavilion wall with an architectural arch recess and ambient sconce. Furthermore, I\'m working on a multi-part beveled frame (`Bronze`, `Walnut`, `Obsidian`, `Alabaster`), a crisp artwork canvas, a brass picture lamp bar, and a mounted museum wall plaque beside the frame. I\'m also creating a dedicated `interactables` array. I\'ve built a 3D turntable preview for the Home / Avatar Studio page.\n\n\n**Developing Museum Experience**\n\nI\'m now building the `Museum3DView` component. I\'m focusing on a full-viewport walkable 3D museum canvas with a non-intrusive collapsible bottom control dock. I\'m prioritizing zero viewport occlusion with solid surfaces, avoiding backdrop-blur over WebGL. I\'m working on a third-person follow camera, and a first-person toggle with WASD controls, drag-to-orbit functionality, and an on-screen mobile D-pad, along with click to inspect artwork.\n\n\n**Refining Modal Functionality**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `UploadArtworkModal.tsx` component, including drag-and-drop file upload with preview, inline form validation, and canvas resize for optimal Firestore storage. I\'m also building the `AccessModal.tsx` to manage museum access, generate access codes, grant direct access by email and role, and manage existing invited visitors. I\'m also working on date sorting, view modes, and metadata display for artwork.\n\n\n**Designing Museum Interactions**\n\nI\'m now detailing the user experience within the museum. The \"Shared Museum\" tab will let users enter via access codes or shared links as their 3D avatars. I\'m developing an `ArtworkInspectModal` for details when artworks are selected. I\'m also creating a home page with 3D avatar preview and customization.\n\n\n"
