    thinking: "**Analyzing Visual Render**\n\nI am thrilled! That screenshot, `.playwright-cli/r1-museum-desktop.png`, is a goldmine. It displays the key rendering features, specifically the \"Sprite\" nameplates. The image provides instant verification. This single visual confirms all my suspicions.\n\n\n**Investigating Render Occlusion**\n\nMy investigation continues into the visibility issues. The `Sprite` materials\' visibility, due to `depthTest: false`, has given me a crucial clue. However, I now understand that solid meshes using `MeshStandardMaterial` are not visible or are occluded. I\'m focusing on the camera\'s initial position relative to the South Wall in `Museum3DView.tsx`.\n\n\n**Examining Avatar Visibility**\n\nI\'ve shifted my attention to `AvatarPreview3D.tsx`, and I\'m really getting somewhere. The avatar\'s invisibility is baffling, and I\'m analyzing lines 107-116. I am now working through the cylinder\'s geometry calculations and camera position relative to the niche. I\'ve noted a potential overlap, and I suspect a key culprit.\n\n\n**Unraveling Rendering Mystery**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a critical discrepancy. My focus is entirely on why the avatar renders as expected in one scenario, but is invisible in another. I\'ve re-examined the `AvatarPreview3D.tsx` file, and I need to resolve why the cylinder renders. My analysis of lines 176\342\200\223217 has highlighted the useEffects are causing an issue. I\'ve switched my focus and analyzed the render for `r1-home-desktop.png`, and noticed a uniform box.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Display Issues**\n\nI\'m now completely focused on the missing \"Drag to rotate\" badge and the avatar\'s unusual positioning. I see now that the viewport height is clipping the badge, while the avatar is inexplicably absent from the upper 700 pixels. The canvas\'s dimensions (606x814) also raise red flags, being completely solid and devoid of expected elements. My attention is now on the canvas\'s properties. I am working through the computations and render requests, which is very promising!\n\n\n**Uncovering Visual Anomalies**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a critical discrepancy! I see that I was right about the background color; the canvas properties are the key. The canvas\'s solid background in the `r1-home-desktop.png` screenshot vs the \"fog\" in `r1-museum-desktop.png` is essential. The `FogExp2` in `Museum3DView.tsx` creates the effect, and the missing elements are obscured by the fog! The camera\'s starting position in the museum view is critical.\n\n\n**Debugging Render Sequence**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the render sequence on SwiftShader, and I believe I\'ve pinpointed a crucial issue! The camera\'s initial `z` position of `7.6` places it between the south wall and the rest of the museum, which is a major issue! I\'m now testing what `gl.getError()` or shader compilation is doing, looking at `webglUtils.ts`. My analysis suggests the `camera.z` gets progressively closer.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Render Synchronization**\n\nI\'ve made a breakthrough. Analyzing the render sequence and the `useEffect` order is crucial. I realized that in `Museum3DView.tsx`, the initial render call happens *before* `buildMuseumArchitecture` has added the museum elements. This is a critical observation! Now I\'m focusing on the `isSoftwareGl` conditional in lines 366-374, particularly the `afterDraw(tick)` function, and how it syncs render calls.\n\n\n**Clarifying Render Order**\n\nMy focus is now on the precise rendering of the South Wall\'s inner face, as seen in `r1-museum-desktop.png`. I\'m examining the `camera`\'s `z` position. The visual clue of the diagonal plaster shading is a huge indication! The crucial detail is that the wall is visible *because* the camera is *inside the museum*. The camera\'s initial starting position in `tick()` at `z = 7.6` is confirmed. I am going back to analyze `Museum3DView.tsx` Line 319.\n\n\n**Verifying Material Visibility**\n\nI\'ve hit a roadblock. I\'m now examining the `MeshStandardMaterial` rendering behavior across the two scenes, and it looks like I need to look at both the three.js version and the environment setup. I am considering the use of `RoomEnvironment` and its interactions with `MeshStandardMaterial`, after noticing that these materials appear completely invisible or solid in both renders. I need to run a quick three.js diagnostic.\n\n\n"
