  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Discovering Two Flaws**\n\nI\'ve got it! I\'ve pinpointed two bugs in the codebase. The first, in `src/three/museumBuilder.ts` and `Museum3DView.tsx`, involves a potential issue in how player z-position is handled within the `Museum3DView` component. It appears the z position isn\'t being properly considered, as well as a possible typo on the line after.\n\n\n**Analyzing Camera Positioning**\n\nI\'ve been examining the camera positioning in detail now. It seems the initial camera setup placed it behind the south wall, which is strange. I\'m focusing on the `clampPlayerPosition` function in `Museum3DView.tsx` and the `tick()` function to understand why the camera remains in this undesirable position, and why the image of the south wall still displayed after 2 seconds. Also, I am investigating Three.js\' `RoomEnvironment()` and the rendering logic in `AvatarPreview3D.tsx`, particularly line 183.\n\n\n**Investigating Rendering Behavior**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into the rendering logic within `AvatarPreview3D.tsx`. I\'ve identified the use of a `PMREMGenerator` for environmental textures and am looking closely at the `afterDrawScheduler` and the `requestRender` function, particularly lines 145\342\200\223155. I\'m trying to figure out the reason why the preview was showing a blank screen even after a delay.\n\n\n**Examining Cylinder Geometry**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a potential issue within `AvatarPreview3D.tsx`, specifically at line 108. I\'m scrutinizing the `CylinderGeometry` parameters, particularly `thetaStart` and `thetaLength`, within the context of the camera\'s perspective. It appears the backside rendering is potentially counter-intuitive, and I\'m still trying to understand it. I think I\'ve also found an edge case.\n\n\n**Uncovering Rendering Discrepancies**\n\nI\'ve made a breakthrough. Examining the behavior of `THREE.WebGLRenderer` in version 0.187.0 with `RoomEnvironment` and `PMREMGenerator` reveals a crucial discrepancy between `AvatarPreview3D.tsx` and `Museum3DView.tsx`. Specifically, I am focusing on the shading and rendering behavior of the cylinder geometry, which is a key difference. I am now trying to understand why this leads to the different solid colors. I am now looking at the camera position as well.\n\n\n**Pinpointing the Root Cause**\n\nI\'ve got it! The camera wasn\'t *behind* the south wall at all, it was inside the museum, facing Julian Vance and Clara Moreau! However, every `MeshStandardMaterial` in the scene was either invisible or rendered as a solid off-white color. I need to understand why. I\'m checking `src/three/museumBuilder.ts` line 206 and `src/three/avatarBuilder.ts` for any clues. I\'m looking into why `r1-museum-desktop.png` and `r1-home-desktop.png` are mostly bright off-white, except for the `Sprite`s. The potential issue might be with how `pmrem.fromScene(new RoomEnvironment(), 0.04)` is handled within `AvatarPreview3D.tsx` and whether the resulting texture gets disposed of or corrupted.\n\n\n**Unraveling Rendering Mysteries**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a perplexing issue. I\'ve noticed a faint silhouette in `r1-museum-desktop.png`, suggesting a problem with `MeshStandardMaterial`.  It\'s almost 100% solid bright off-white. The same issue manifested in `AvatarPreview3D`. Both scenarios share these elements: the `PMREMGenerator` and `detectSoftwareGl()` resulting in `afterDraw`. Yet, `SpriteMaterial` renders perfectly. This points towards a specific incompatibility. I\'ll focus on `renderer.render(scene, camera)` in both views now, as well as line 206 and `detectSoftwareGl()`. I\'m starting a diagnostic run now.\n\n\n"
