    thinking: "**Discovering Red-Handed Bugs!**\n\nI\'ve just stumbled upon not one, but *two* critical bugs! The first culprit is a blank `AvatarPreview3D`. It\'s all because the PMREM generator was being created without any context! Check out `src/components/AvatarPreview3D.tsx`, where I\'ve identified this blunder.\n\n\n**Analyzing Environment Context**\n\nOkay, I\'ve got a significant update. While investigating the environment texture context, I noticed a disposal issue. The `pmrem.dispose()` call wasn\'t the problem, but I discovered a lingering `centerPx` set to `[0,0,0,0]` \342\200\223 it was never cleared, leaving alpha at zero! Also, the `ResizeObserver` was activated on mount, a potential timing issue. The problem, I think, is just starting to reveal itself!\n\n\n**Unraveling Rendering Mysteries**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the beige screenshot issue in `AvatarPreview3D`. It\'s not a readPixels problem, nor the preserveDrawingBuffer. I\'ve tracked the rendering calls and found that `renderer.setSize()` is the culprit; it resets the canvas. Now, the `handleResize` call does trigger a redraw, but something\'s still preventing the initial `#EBE6DD` background from persisting after the swap. I need to understand why `requestRender` isn\'t redrawing after the resize.\n\n\n**Investigating Color & Geometry**\n\nI\'m now honing in on `AvatarPreview3D.tsx` lines 108-117, and I\'ve found a crucial clue. A niche geometry with `#f2ede4` color is being used. Then I looked at the screenshots `r1-home-desktop.png` and `r1-museum-desktop.png`. I found that the background color of the left panel in `r1-home-desktop.png` exactly matches the intended background color. However, I noticed a subtle fog cloud gradient in the screenshots!\n\n\n**Pinpointing Mesh Visibility**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on why meshes disappear in `Museum3DView`, mirroring the visibility issue in `AvatarPreview3D`. It\'s not the lighting, but shared lines between the two files are key. In `Museum3DView.tsx`, I am seeing that dozens of meshes aren\'t visible, with only two Sprites showing up. I am digging through shared code to identify the common factor causing this baffling issue of complete mesh invisibility.\n\n\n**Uncovering Material Render Failures**\n\nI\'ve made a breakthrough! It seems that `MeshStandardMaterial` isn\'t rendering in either `AvatarPreview3D` or `Museum3DView`. Only `SpriteMaterial` is showing up, which is a massive clue. My attention has turned to the camera position and `playerRig` in `Museum3DView.tsx`. It turns out the player is inside the museum, which may be the cause of this rendering failure!\n\n\n**Examining Scene Geometry**\n\nOkay, things are getting clearer. I\'ve been comparing camera and object positions between `AvatarPreview3D` and the museum scene. The avatar camera is set at `(0.55, 1.48, 3.65)`, whereas, in the museum, I have found that I am inside the building! This discrepancy with camera positions is a likely cause. Moreover, I\'ve confirmed that `isSoftwareGl` is true and that the `RoomEnvironment` is being initialized correctly, without console errors. However, there are rendering inconsistencies between the two scenes.\n\n\n**Clarifying Texture Visibility**\n\nI\'ve made a huge discovery! After re-examining `r1-home-desktop.png` and `r1-museum-desktop.png`, I\'ve realized `MeshStandardMaterial` *is* rendering. `r1-museum-desktop.png` clearly shows the Venetian plaster texture filling the entire frame. The key question now is, what wall is rendering in front of the camera, obscuring the intended content? This changes everything!\n\n\n**Analyzing Camera Z Position**\n\nOkay, the issue is becoming crystal clear! The camera is starting behind the south wall! Examining `Museum3DView.tsx`, the `southWall` is at `z = 6.7`, but the camera starts at `z = 7.6`. The software GL renderer might be interfering due to the `afterDraw` scheduler and `renderer.setSize()`. It seems that at `t = 0`, the camera is behind the wall, which means at tick zero, the camera is still behind the wall! I must check this out!\n\n\n**Investigating Frame Zero Issues**\n\nI\'m now honing in on frame zero! The camera\'s initial position is behind the south wall and its z coordinate hasn\'t been updated yet. I\'m focusing on why the `tick()` function may never be running in `Museum3DView`, and if `requestRender()` has never ran its second frame in `AvatarPreview3D` too! The issue may lie within `createAfterDrawScheduler` in `src/three/webglUtils.ts`, which I will carefully check out. I\'m onto something!\n\n\n**Unveiling Render Sequencing**\n\nOkay, I\'ve got a crucial observation! It seems the order of `useEffect` executions in `Museum3DView` may explain the initial render. The South Wall *was* visible in the first frame, and `tick()` *did* run at least once, which explains why the sprites show up. I\'m focusing on why the viewport is filled with the South Wall in `r1-museum-desktop.png` and solid color in `AvatarPreview3D`. It\342\200\231s probably related to camera positions. I will examine `AvatarPreview3D.tsx` and `Museum3DView.tsx` and camera positions after one second.\n\n\n\n**Examining Geometry Conflicts**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into `AvatarPreview3D` to understand why the niche geometry seems to be obscuring the view, initially. I was mistaken about it being in front, its position is actually `z = -0.4`, while the avatar is at `-3.8`. Line 74 (`controls.update()`) might be key. Also, there\'s a crucial order-of-execution issue between building the avatar rig and the cleanup function, in lines 183-190. I will keep checking!\n\n\n\n**Confirming Wall Obstruction**\n\nI\'ve just made a crucial breakthrough! I\'m now certain the initial render in `Museum3DView` and the solid color in `AvatarPreview3D` are due to the south wall, and I\'ve confirmed that the wall is positioned at the wrong location! It is because of a conflict. In `MuseumBuilder.ts`, the south wall is at `layout.southWallZ + 0.2`, yet the `Museum3DView` camera starts right behind it. I have some debugging to do!\n\n\n\n"
