  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Discovering Code Flaws**\n\nI\'ve made a breakthrough! My attention zeroed in on `src/three/avatarBuilder.ts` and, with a quick review, I\'ve pinpointed two geometry bugs. The man\'s hair cap was covering his face due to a specific issue on line 496. These issues were identified in mere seconds.\n\n\n**Refining Geometry Adjustments**\n\nI\'ve been meticulously refining the geometry adjustments and have zeroed in on the details of the avatar\'s hair. Specifically, the sphere geometry, previously causing the forehead to be covered, is now resolved with a tilt, modified `thetaLength`, and strategic positioning. I\'ve also added a back-of-head hair shell to ensure a clean, crisp, and sculpted appearance. I will begin to analyze the next point of interest.\n\n\n**Analyzing Dress Geometry**\n\nI\'ve got the dress geometry under scrutiny now. The current `LatheGeometry` setup has a few flaws. The single-sided nature means the interior hem is invisible from the back. I\'ve noted a radius issue, especially at the hip and thigh. My proposed clean fix involves making the dress material double-sided, narrowing the hip spread and thigh radius, and making the dress flare more generously from the waist to the hem, incorporating updated dress points and scale adjustments.\n\n\n**Adjusting Skirt and Lighting**\n\nI\'m making further refinements. For skirted outfits, I\'m adjusting the leg cylinder position and width to prevent clipping, setting a leg swing limit of 0.36 radians. I\'ve also incorporated a sculpted underside cap and shoulder blend for the Woman Gallery Dress. Moving on, I\'m now fine-tuning museum lighting and atmosphere. The current setup is over-exposed; I\'m adjusting the tone mapping exposure, adjusting the hemisphere light and key directional lighting, and reducing point light intensity to achieve crisp contrast. I\'m focusing on `Museum3DView.tsx` and `museumBuilder.ts` to implement these changes, particularly lines 189, 201, 216, 219 and 236.\n\n\n"
