    thinking: "**Spotting Critical Detail**\n\nI\'ve hit a potential snag. I was examining the component interactions, specifically comparing `Museum3DView.tsx` with `AvatarPreview3D.tsx`. A glaring inconsistency jumped out: the `alpha` parameter is handled differently between the two. The `AvatarPreview3D.tsx` file passes `alpha: false`, but I must examine line 375 of the `Museum3DView.tsx` file to see if this is intentional or if there is some other conflict. This could be significant.\n\n\n**Analyzing Render Behavior**\n\nI\'ve uncovered a crucial detail. I found that the `alpha` parameter is explicitly not being passed as `false` in `Museum3DView.tsx`, and that `scene.background` is rendered with `alpha = 1`, regardless. This leads me to question why `gl.readPixels` is returning `[0, 0, 0, 0]` despite a clear color of `[0, 0, 0, 1]`. The differences between `r2` and `r2b` now point to discrepancies in lines 191/192 and 207 of `Museum3DView.tsx`, specifically regarding fog and renderer settings, which leads me to suspect `onResize` or `setSize` might be called after a render.\n\n\n**Investigating Render Loop**\n\nOkay, here\'s the current state of my analysis. I\'m focusing on the render loop, specifically within `Museum3DView.tsx`. I was looking at lines 533-537 and 552-561, plus line 183 to see if `isSoftwareGl` is false, and how that influences `requestAnimationFrame`. Then I considered the diagnostic data, which confirmed I should expect `isSoftwareGl` to be false because \"rendererStr\": \"WebKit WebGL\". Now I\'m checking `webglUtils.ts` line 15 to assess possible influences.\n\n\n**Uncovering Render Issues**\n\nI\'ve made a breakthrough. I discovered `detectSoftwareGl()` returns true on SwiftShader, which helps explain the render anomalies. I\'m now comparing lines 531 and 556 in `Museum3DView.tsx` with lines from `r2` to understand the differences in output. My focus has shifted to lines 600-632 to see *when* and *why* these occur.\n\n\n**Examining Mount Order**\n\nMy analysis has progressed. I\'m scrutinizing the mount order of `Museum3DView`, specifically how the scene is populated over time. I noted that `renderer.render(scene, camera)` occurs with an empty scene during the first effect, then again only after the later effects add content. I\'m focusing on the timing of shader compilation and if it\'s causing the initial pixel value of `[0, 0, 0, 0]`. I am trying to figure out if shader compilation delay explains why the pixel data from the initial render call on line 556 is zero, when the scene data is there later.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Canvas Wipe**\n\nI\'ve hit a potential breakthrough! I\'ve been reviewing `Museum3DView.tsx` again and I\'ve honed in on lines 211\342\200\223214 and the `PMREMGenerator`. Its usage of `pmrem.fromScene` and `renderer.setRenderTarget(null)` has sparked a new line of thinking. Could a subsequent clear, possibly triggered by a canvas resize, be wiping the initial render? I need to explore how and when the canvas is cleared, particularly after the scene\'s initial render on line 556. I\'m focusing on why pixel color returned `[0, 0, 0, 0]`. I think the rendering of a new room environment is impacting render output.\n\n\n**Refining Render Strategy**\n\nI\'ve got a new approach. I\'m modifying the DOM directly with `mountRef.current?.setAttribute(\'data-frames\', String(frameCount))` on the `Museum3DView` root div to reduce overhead, I\'m now explicitly passing `alpha: false` to `WebGLRenderer` and setting the clear color, and I\'m restoring `scene.fog = new THREE.FogExp2(fogHex, 0.011)`. The plan also includes calling `renderer.render(scene, camera)` after `buildMuseumArchitecture` in Effect 2 and in `onResize`, with the goal of preventing an `[0, 0, 0, 0]` pixel color.\n\n\n"
