  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Discovering Massive Gaps**\n\nI\'m incredibly excited! I\'ve just identified **four** significant bugs and UX gaps by carefully reviewing `App.tsx`, `layout.ts`, and `Museum3DView.tsx`. Now, I\'m focusing on dissecting each one to fully understand the implications. The first, particularly glaring, bug is the **WHY** bug.\n\n\n**Analyzing Keyboard Control**\n\nI\'ve got a critical issue to address: the user can\'t freely navigate the museum using the keyboard! Pressing \'W\' or an arrow key immediately results in a collision with the bench in `Museum3DView.tsx`. Focus management is also a problem. Clicking \"Enter 3D Museum\" or a dock button keeps focus on the button. This prevents smooth walking and can scroll the page instead.\n\n\n**Refining Museum Navigation**\n\nI\'m now iterating on the museum\'s keyboard controls. Specifically, I realized the arrow keys currently only strafe. I\'m experimenting with a hybrid approach that integrates WASD keys, creating an intuitive movement scheme where users can walk forward/backward with W/S and turn or strafe with A/D or arrow keys. I\'m focusing on ensuring both control methods feel natural, regardless of what the user is expecting!\n\n\n**Improving Keyboard Control**\n\nI\'m now implementing smoother turning with `ArrowLeft`, `ArrowRight`, `Q`, and `E` keys, allowing complete museum navigation via keyboard. I\'m also addressing the bench collision and sliding. Specifically, I\'m adjusting the player\'s position near benches and introducing an auto-glide behavior when walking into them to create a smoother, more natural experience. I\'m also adding a sprint function with the `Shift` key to allow walking faster through the expanded wings.\n\n\n**Optimizing Bench Placement**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on optimizing bench placement. My goal is to allow smooth keyboard navigation throughout the museum, even when encountering benches. I\'m exploring two approaches: either slightly narrowing the bench collision boxes and implementing smooth corner-sliding around them when walking directly into a bench, or strategically placing the benches in alternate bays. I\'m prioritizing not breaking any existing unit tests, so I\'ll check `layout.test.ts` before making any changes.\n\n\n**Uncovering a Critical Bug**\n\nWow! I can\'t believe I missed this. While reviewing `src/museum/layout.test.ts`, I revisited `App.tsx` and uncovered a massive bug! Lines 106 and 435 were the key. `inspectState` is an object, so `Boolean(inspectState)` always evaluates to `true`. This caused `isPaused` to always be true, freezing keyboard walking and explaining the rendering issues with `r2b`, `r2c`, and `r2d`. Thank goodness we caught this!\n\n\n**Clarifying Image Storage**\n\nI\'ve been meticulously examining the image upload process. I discovered that when a user is signed in with Google, images are saved as optimized JPEGs directly within Firebase Firestore. Specifically, they\'re stored in the `museums/{museumId}/artworks/{artworkId}` collection path, in the `imageUrl` field, alongside other metadata. This resolves the question of persistent storage for images. I\'m moving on to ensure this process is thoroughly documented.\n\n\n**Verifying Cloud Storage**\n\nI\'m now diving into the specifics of image storage. I\'m focusing on ensuring data URL length limits work properly in Firestore, checking `firestore.rules` and `firebase-blueprint.json`. I\'ve uncovered a potential issue in Guest Studio mode. There is a `localStorage` quota and error handling that is not explicit to the user.\n\n\n"
    message_id: "KdcBa53mIpytmtkPmruDuQw"
