  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Requirements Deeply**\n\nI\'m incredibly impressed by the clarity of these requirements. My initial focus is translating each one meticulously and thoroughly. I\'m taking the first requirement, \"Bug Fix \342\200\224 Strict Per-User Data,\" and breaking it down into its core components to fully understand the user\'s intent. The goal is to avoid misinterpretation and potential problems.\n\n\n**Isolating the Root Cause**\n\nI\'ve traced the issue back to `App.tsx` and identified the crucial problem. The code\'s fallback to `guestMuseum` upon user sign-in, specifically when `cloudMuseums` are empty or `ensurePersonalMuseum` fails, led to data being erroneously written to `localStorage`. Upon sign-out, this persisted as guest data, explaining the user data leakage. Now, let\'s explore why `ensurePersonalMuseum` is failing!\n\n\n**Investigating Further Security**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a critical security flaw. I see that `guestArtworks` is vulnerable to data persistence from signed-in sessions, even after sign-out. The issue is exacerbated by the `museums` default `isOpenByCode: true`, which potentially exposes private data. The default must be changed to `isOpenByCode: false` to ensure museums are private unless explicitly set to public. When signing out, I\'ll need to reset all cloud-related data and keep `guestArtworks` strictly isolated.\n\n\n**Refining Sign-In/Sign-Out**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a critical fix: preventing any reliance on `guestArtworks` when a user is signed in. The goal is to always present a loading state or error message if Firestore data isn\'t immediately available, never falling back to shared `localStorage`. I\'m also reviewing `firestore.rules` and `museumService.ts` for potential issues during new user sign-ins, paying close attention to data integrity.\n\n**Re-Evaluating Room Architecture**\n\nMy focus has shifted to the architectural design, building distinct, enclosed museum rooms. The idea is to create a multi-room setup, with each room holding exactly eight walls. Adding an artwork should automatically trigger the creation of a new room with a new set of eight walls, enhancing the structure significantly.\n\n\n**Constructing Room Architectures**\n\nI\'m now implementing the architectural rules for the rooms. Each room is designed to hold exactly eight artworks, and I\'ve established the logic to automatically construct new rooms as the artwork count grows. The architecture cycles, ensuring each room has a unique design. Room 1 will be octagonal, Room 2 a circular, etc. I\'m focusing on ensuring artwork frames fit cleanly within these spaces, with no overlap, specifically taking the octagonal rotunda architecture into account.\n\n\n**Designing Room Doorways**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the museum room doorways, and ensuring we get eight crisp artwork walls in every room. Every room is designed with the dimensions width=16m, depth=18m, including a 3.2m-wide, 3.8m-high doorway at the North (connecting to the next room). The eight artwork walls are: three on the West wall (at z = +4.8, 0, -4.8), three on the East wall (at z = +4.8, 0, -4.8), and two flanking the North doorway (at x = -4.6 and +4.6).\n\n\n**Defining Room Elements**\n\nI\'m now focusing on ensuring that each artwork wall aligns perfectly within the defined museum room, while simultaneously avoiding any doorway overlap. Furthermore, I\'m working to make the architectural elements in each room truly distinct, not just in terms of shape but also the ceiling and flooring inlays and the centerpiece decor. I\'m exploring specific designs: a Chamfered Rotunda Hall, a Botanical Atrium Gallery, and a Classical Salon Room.\n\n\n**Implementing Room Doors**\n\nI\'m now fully focused on creating interactive doorways to connect each museum room! I\'m adding realistic 3D double-leaf doors with walnut frames, gilded bronze accents, and brass handles for a premium feel. These doors will physically block passage when closed, but smoothly open with a `rotation.y` transformation. Furthermore, I\'ll add keyboard shortcuts or prompts for interaction.\n\n\n**Adding Door Interaction**\n\nI\'m now implementing the room doorways to be interactive and intuitive. I will make a smooth animation on the 3D double doors that swing open and block user passage when they are closed. I\'m adding `F`, `Space`, `Enter` and clickable interactions to the doors, with different text prompts depending on their state. The initial room will have a \"Next Room\" message that appears until a certain number of artworks are added.\n\n\n**Adding Room Name Signs**\n\nI\'m now implementing interactive and editable room name plaques mounted above each doorway! I\'ve created sculpted gilded-bronze and dark-walnut 3D lintels that display the custom room name. Clicking the 3D signboard opens an inline rename modal, and the changes are applied to the 3D signboard texture in real-time. I\'m also ensuring the HUD updates with the new room name.\n\n\n**Adding Room Furniture**\n\nI\'m now focusing on unique decor for each room to make them truly distinct. I\'m adding curated furniture, starting with procedural generation for four distinct flower vases, each with unique materials and shapes like ceramic, porcelain, terracotta, and bronze. Each vase will be paired with varying arrangements of stems, petals, and leaves, and complemented by corner potted botanicals and room-specific pedestals. I will also incorporate specialized ceiling features to enhance the aesthetics.\n\n\n"
    message_id: "1N0Ba43WAqOcqtsP7rf6mAU"
    tool_calls {
