  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Security Concerns**\n\nI\'m deep-diving into the security implications of those recent database changes. I\'m scrutinizing `firebase-blueprint.json`, `firestore.rules`, and `museumService.ts` with extreme care. The goal? Iron-clad per-user isolation. I\'m focusing particularly on the `museumService.ts` file, and making sure the \"create\" process adheres to this fundamental security principle. I\'m going through security with a fine-toothed comb!\n\n\n**Refining User Privacy**\n\nI\'m now prioritizing private-by-default museum creation. I\'ve ensured a new user\'s museum is initially closed, setting `isOpenByCode: false`. The key is absolute user separation, so no one can discover another\'s museum. The signed-in user gets an empty gallery ready for their own personal photos. Starter samples? They remain exclusively in Guest Preview, ensuring user data privacy!\n\n\n**Implementing User-Specific Storage**\n\nI\'m now implementing the image upload process! The focus is sharp: prevent any mingling of guest and user data. In `App.tsx`, I\'m working to ensure that when a user is signed in, no guest data ever pollutes their private cloud data. I\'m focusing on the `cloudReady` state; while loading, the screen shows a clear message. Upon sign-out, I\'m meticulously wiping all user-specific state and restoring the Guest Preview to its pristine default. The goal? Utter separation of all users\' data.\n\n\n**Validating Secure Data Persistence**\n\nI\'m now focusing on robust security rules for custom room names. I\'m prioritizing the optional `roomNames` field as a Firestore `map`. I\'m working to make the data secure with these updates: I\'m implementing a size bound of 30 items for any `roomNames` map. This is to ensure secure data storage. The goal is to make sure existing documents don\'t break, so I\'m making this a seamless migration. I\'m verifying the security with `allow update` and checking affected keys.\n\n\n"
    message_id: "I94Ba7G8MfOkqtsP8_qKwQI"
