  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing the Layout Engine**\n\nI\'ve successfully deployed the rules! My current focus is designing and implementing the Multi-Room Architecture\'s pure math engine, specifically within `/src/museum/layout.ts`. I plan to write comprehensive unit tests in `/src/museum/layout.test.ts`. I will carefully examine all spatial dimensions to ensure accuracy.\n\n\n**Defining Room Geometry**\n\nI\'m now focusing on defining the room parameters to ensure zero overlap and seamless navigation. I\'ve set `ARTWORKS_PER_ROOM` to 8, which dictates that each room has 8 walls. The `MIN_ROOMS` is set to 1. Room dimensions are defined: `ROOM_WIDTH`, `ROOM_DEPTH`, and `HALL_HEIGHT`. I\'ve calculated the room center based on the room index (`r`), and am now focused on verifying the player spawn position and initial camera placement within Room 0, centered at `z=0`.\n\n\n**Calculating Artwork Slots**\n\nI\'ve determined the precise coordinates for the eight artwork slots within each room, ensuring a balanced distribution. Specifically, I am focusing on Room `r` and have established the center at `cz = -r * ROOM_DEPTH`. My calculations include precise `x`, `z`, and `rotY` values, alongside the `standPoint` for optimal viewing, for each slot: `West Wall South`, `East Wall South`, `West Wall Center`, `East Wall Center`, `West Wall North`, `East Wall North`, `North Flank West`, and `North Flank East`.\n\n\n**Verifying Spacing and Positioning**\n\nNow I\'m diving into spacing verification for the artwork slots. I\'ve double-checked all wall positions, confirming the north wall\'s total width and confirming the West and East walls. The center-to-center spacing is precise, allowing ample breathing room and preventing clipping. The checks confirm the accuracy of my calculations. I am meticulously reviewing each artwork slot\'s coordinates and their respective standpoints for optimal viewing angles.\n\n\n**Refining Spatial Boundaries**\n\nI\'m now refining the spatial boundaries, particularly around the doorways, and have confirmed that artwork slots have ample space. I\'ve placed the doorway with `x \342\210\210 [-1.7, +1.7]` and have calculated space in West wall. I am moving on to designing the **4 Distinct Room Shapes**, specifically the `rotunda` and `atrium`. My next goal is to work on defining all the parameters for these spaces.\n\n\n**Defining Room Styles**\n\nI\'m now focusing on defining the distinct styles for each room to enhance their aesthetic appeal. I am excited to introduce unique themes: Room 1 will feature **Branches & Golden Pampas**, and Room 2 will showcase a **Cobalt & Gold Chinoiserie Baluster Vase with Crimson & Blush Peonies**. Room 3 will highlight a **Brushed Bronze Ribbed Vessel with Sculptural White Orchids & Eucalyptus**. The following rooms will cycle through these diverse styles. I am now working on **Interactive Double-Leaf 3D Doors** for each room transition, ensuring their identification with IDs and directional properties.\n\n\n**Constructing 3D Signboards**\n\nI\'m now designing the interactive 3D signboards for each room transition. On the south side, the sign displays the next room\'s name, and on the north side, it displays the previous. For Room 0, I\'ll mount a decorative entrance door with a signboard. The final room will feature a gallery expansion portal with a signboard. I\'m focusing on ensuring these signboards are clickable and dynamically update with the correct room indices.\n\n\n**Visualizing Interactive Elements**\n\nI\'m now visualizing the interactive elements for intuitive navigation. Upon entering Room 1, I see the grand doorway. Above it is the clickable Room Name Signboard. When the player adds a 9th artwork, the North door becomes an openable passage into Room 2! Even with just 1 room, the player sees a Rotunda, a door with a Signboard, and connecting doors that are openable with `F` or `Space`.\n\n\n**Implementing Interactive Prompts**\n\nI\'m now implementing interactive prompts and actions for room transitions. I will focus on the architectural door for the final room: I\'ll design it to display a clear prompt (\"Add more than 8 artworks to unlock Room II\") when clicked or approached. Additionally, all connecting doors between rooms will physically swing open or closed on user interaction. I am making all room signboards clickable, allowing room renaming. For Room 2, the door from Room 1 will show Room 2\'s name on its south face.\n\n\n**Refining Signboard Functionality**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the dynamic display and interactivity of the signboards for room transitions. I\'ve confirmed that walking up to Door 1 from Room 1 shows Room 2\'s name, and I can rename Room 2 by clicking it. Also, the north face of Room 1\'s entrance shows Room 1\'s name, which can also be renamed. Plus, the final room\'s North portal will always display the Final Room\'s name. I\'ve also implemented a \"Rename Room\" button in the HUD to streamline room renaming from anywhere.\n\n\n**Verifying Boundary Constraints**\n\nI\'m now deep in the details, meticulously verifying the interior bounds of each room to ensure smooth player movement and collision detection. I\'m focusing on the `minX`, `maxX`, and north/south wall constraints, as well as those for partitioning walls between rooms. I\'m paying close attention to the player\'s interaction with open and closed doorways at `door.z`, ensuring they can move through open doorways but are blocked by closed partitions, while carefully managing the `DOOR_WALL_BUFFER` to clamp players at doorways correctly.\n\n\n"
