  view_file {
    absolute_path_uri: "file:///skills/system_skills/firebase-integration-rpc/SKILL.md"
    end_line: 341
    content: "---\nname: \"firebase-integration-rpc\"\ndescription: |\n  Integrates Firebase (Firestore and Auth) into applications, generate secure rules, and handle errors.\n\n  Use this skill whenever the application requires:\n  * Persistent Data & Database: Storing, querying, and persisting application state, documents, or records across sessions and devices (even if Firebase is not explicitly named).\n  * Multi-Role & Access Control (RBAC): Applications with distinct user roles or permissions (e.g., admins, super admins, managers, employees, assignees, creators, customers).\n  * User Authentication & Accounts: App user sign-in/login, user profiles, credentials, or private user data.\n  * Security Hardening & Rules: Designing schemas, writing/auditing `firestore.rules`, enforcing role validation, or debugging permission errors.\n  * SDK & Tool Setup: Provisioning Firebase via `ProvisionFirebase` RPC, initializing the Firebase SDK in React/TypeScript, or deploying rules via `DeployRules` RPC.\n\n  Do NOT use this skill for:\n  * General GCP resource management outside of the specific Firebase tools provided.\n  * Purely transient or single-session apps with no persistence, user accounts, or shared state (e.g., simple unit converters, local calculators).\n  * Applications explicitly requesting a relational SQL database or SQL-specific tooling, such as PostgreSQL, Postgres, Cloud SQL, Supabase, Neon, RDS, Aurora, MySQL, MariaDB, SQL Server, Drizzle, Prisma, pgvector, RDBMS, or any other relational SQL engine (use cloudsql-setup instead).\n---\n\n<!-- disableFinding(\"Master\") -->\n\n# Firebase Integration Skill\n\nThe Firebase Integration Skill enables the agent to seamlessly incorporate\nFirebase services into applications. It provides a structured approach to data\nmodeling, security rule generation, and error handling, ensuring that\napplications are both functional and secure by default.\n\n## Related Skills (Cloud SQL & Google Maps Platform)\n\n-   **Relational / SQL Databases (`cloudsql-setup`)**: Firebase Firestore is a\n    NoSQL document database. If the user explicitly asks for PostgreSQL,\n    Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQL Server, Cloud SQL, Supabase, Neon, RDS,\n    Aurora, relational tables, SQL queries, foreign keys, RDBMS, or `pg` /\n    Prisma / Drizzle / pgvector\342\200\224**and** `cloudsql-setup`\n    (`/skills/system_skills/cloudsql-setup/SKILL.md`) is listed in your\n    available skills for this session **and** you have not already pivoted to\n    Firebase from `cloudsql-setup` due to Cloud SQL ineligibility or quota\342\200\224**do\n    NOT use this skill**; load `/skills/system_skills/cloudsql-setup/SKILL.md`\n    instead. (If `cloudsql-setup` is not in your available skills, or if you\n    pivoted here from `cloudsql-setup` after an eligibility or quota failure,\n    stay on this Firebase skill\342\200\224you are strictly forbidden from returning to\n    `cloudsql-setup`.)\n-   **Interactive Maps, Places & Routes (`google-maps-platform`)**: If your\n    Firebase-backed app also renders interactive maps, markers, routes,\n    geocoding, or Places search (e.g., store locators, delivery trackers, travel\n    itineraries, or real-estate listings stored in Firestore), load whichever\n    Google Maps skill path is listed in your available skills for this session\n    (`/skills/system_skills/google_maps_platform/SKILL.md` or\n    `/skills/system_skills/google_maps_platform_ghp_integration/SKILL.md`)\n    alongside this skill.\n\n## Reference Files (`view_file` Required Before Writing Blueprint, Rules, Error Handlers, or Admin SDK Code)\n\nTopic                                                    | Reference File                                                                                                                                                                            | When to Read\n:------------------------------------------------------- | :---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | :-----------\n**Blueprint Schema & Hardened Firestore Security Rules** | [references/firestore-rules-and-blueprint.md](references/firestore-rules-and-blueprint.md) (`/skills/system_skills/firebase-integration-rpc/references/firestore-rules-and-blueprint.md`) | **MUST READ** before creating or updating `firebase-blueprint.json`, writing `DRAFT_firestore.rules` / `firestore.rules`, configuring ESLint with `@firebase/eslint-plugin-security-rules`, or running Phases 0\342\200\2236 of the Eight Pillars security rules workflow.\n**Connection Validation & Firestore Error Handlers**     | [references/error-handling-and-sdk.md](references/error-handling-and-sdk.md) (`/skills/system_skills/firebase-integration-rpc/references/error-handling-and-sdk.md`)                      | **MUST READ** when writing the startup `testConnection()` check (`getDocFromServer(doc(db, \'test\', \'connection\'))`) and the `OperationType` / `FirestoreErrorInfo` / `handleFirestoreError` wrapper.\n**Firebase Admin SDK (Server-Side Access)**              | [references/admin-sdk.md](references/admin-sdk.md) (`/skills/system_skills/firebase-integration-rpc/references/admin-sdk.md`)                                                             | **MUST READ** before writing or changing any `firebase-admin` code, when the user asks for the Admin SDK or server-side Firestore / Auth access, or when backend `firebase-admin` calls fail with `PERMISSION_DENIED`.\n\n## Prerequisites and Tool Usage\n\nBefore proceeding with the detailed instructions, ensure you use the appropriate\nRPC actions when needed:\n\n1.  **Provision Firebase (MANDATORY)**:\n\n    Before writing ANY Firebase integration code, you MUST configure Firebase.\n    The Firebase setup process always follows these steps:\n\n    1.  Call `show_aistudio_ui` with `ui_type: \"firebase_setup\"`. *Immediately\n        after calling `show_aistudio_ui`, you MUST STOP the conversation and\n        wait for the user to respond.*\n    2.  When you are resumed after the user interacts with the UI:\n        *   If the user declined Firebase setup, do NOT call any Firebase RPCs\n            or implement Firebase!\n        *   Otherwise, call `rpc_action` with `service_name: \"fax\"`,\n            `method_name: \"GetFirebaseTos\"` to verify that the terms have been\n            accepted. Arguments: None.\n    3.  **Critical Resuming Behavior**: Treat the terms as accepted ONLY if the\n        `GetFirebaseTos` result explicitly contains `\"accepted\": true`. An empty\n        result (`{}`), a result without an `accepted` field, or `\"accepted\":\n        false` all mean the terms have NOT been accepted; never infer acceptance\n        from the absence of an error. If the terms have NOT been accepted, do\n        NOT call `ProvisionFirebase` or implement Firebase: call\n        `show_aistudio_ui` with `ui_type: \"firebase_setup\"` again, STOP, and\n        wait for the user to respond. Only once the terms are accepted, you MUST\n        call `rpc_action` with `service_name: \"fax\"`, `method_name:\n        \"ProvisionFirebase\"` to provision the database. Arguments for\n        `ProvisionFirebase`:\n\n        *   `platform`: `web` or `android` (required)\n        *   `project_id`: the existing Firebase / Google Cloud project ID to\n            provision into. Required when the user named a project and on repeat\n            calls; see [Project Selection](#project-selection).\n        *   `firestore_region`: (optional)\n        *   `database_id`: (optional)\n\n        > [!CAUTION] You MUST call the `ProvisionFirebase` RPC (via\n        > `rpc_action`) after verifying terms acceptance on resume before\n        > performing any other task (such as writing integration code, modifying\n        > files, building, or linting). If you skip this, the database will not\n        > be provisioned and the application will fail.\n\n2.  **Deploy Firebase Rules**:\n\n    *   When you need to deploy rules (e.g., changes made to `firestore.rules`),\n        call `rpc_action` with `service_name: \"fax\"`, `method_name:\n        \"DeployRules\"`. Arguments: None.\n\n### Project Selection\n\n> [!IMPORTANT] **Honor the user\'s project.** If the user named a Firebase or\n> Google Cloud project ID anywhere in the conversation (for example \"use the\n> existing Firebase project `my-project-123`\"), you **MUST** pass it verbatim as\n> `project_id` on every `ProvisionFirebase` call. If you omit `project_id`, the\n> backend silently picks one of the user\'s other projects, and the app ends up\n> wired to a project the user did not choose. On the first call, omit\n> `project_id` only when the user has not named a project; repeat calls follow\n> the rules below.\n\n*   `project_id` must be the project **ID** (lowercase letters, digits, and\n    hyphens, as shown in the Firebase console URL), not a display name or\n    project number. The backend uses the value literally. If the user gave only\n    a display name or number, ask them for the project ID before calling\n    `ProvisionFirebase`.\n*   Do not tell the user that Firebase is being set up in a specific project\n    unless you are passing that project as `project_id`.\n*   On repeat `ProvisionFirebase` calls, pass the same `project_id` you used\n    originally. If the app was provisioned earlier without one, pass the\n    `projectId` already in `firebase-applet-config.json`, unless it is the remix\n    placeholder `remixed-project-id`.\n\nIf you passed `project_id`, confirm the returned `firebase_project_id` (or\n`firebaseProjectId`) and `projectId` in `firebase-applet-config.json` match it.\nIf they differ, **stop and report both IDs to the user**. The backend has\nalready rewritten `firebase-applet-config.json` for the provisioned project, so\ndo not hand-edit it or write integration code against the wrong project.\n\nIf `ProvisionFirebase` fails while targeting the user\'s requested project,\nreport the error to the user verbatim and ask how to proceed. Never fall back to\ncalling `ProvisionFirebase` without `project_id`, and never recommend keeping a\nproject the user did not ask for.\n\n## Firebase Admin SDK (Server-Side Access)\n\nThis skill builds on the client-side Firebase SDK with Firestore security rules.\nThat is the default and it works on every project. Do NOT add the Firebase Admin\nSDK (`firebase-admin`) on your own initiative.\n\nWhen the user asks for the Firebase Admin SDK or for server-side Firestore /\nAuth access, or when backend code that already uses `firebase-admin` fails with\n`7 PERMISSION_DENIED` or HTTP 403, you **MUST** read\n[references/admin-sdk.md](references/admin-sdk.md)\n(`/skills/system_skills/firebase-integration-rpc/references/admin-sdk.md`) using\n`view_file` **before** writing or changing any Admin SDK code. Admin SDK\noperations that need IAM (server-side Firestore access, Auth user management,\ntoken revocation checks) are not available on Google-hosted (Starter Tier)\nprojects, so that reference starts with a project ownership check. Verifying an\nID token with `verifyIdToken(token)` needs no IAM and works on any project.\n\n## 1. Set up firebase-blueprint.json\n\nBefore writing any application or security-rules code, you **MUST** read\n[references/firestore-rules-and-blueprint.md](references/firestore-rules-and-blueprint.md)\n(`/skills/system_skills/firebase-integration-rpc/references/firestore-rules-and-blueprint.md`)\nusing `view_file` and create or update `firebase-blueprint.json` in the project\nroot:\n\n-   Represent the application\'s data structures (`entities`, JSON Schema\n    definitions) and collection paths (`firestore`) in `firebase-blueprint.json`\n    following the `FirebaseBlueprintConfig` interface in\n    `references/firestore-rules-and-blueprint.md`.\n-   Keep `firebase-blueprint.json` synchronized whenever your data model\n    changes.\n\n## Validate Connection to Firestore\n\nRead\n[references/error-handling-and-sdk.md](references/error-handling-and-sdk.md)\n(`/skills/system_skills/firebase-integration-rpc/references/error-handling-and-sdk.md`)\nusing `view_file` and include the `testConnection()` startup check\n(`getDocFromServer(doc(db, \'test\', \'connection\'))`) to verify Firestore\nconnectivity and catch `client is offline` configuration errors early.\n\n## 2. Generate Firestore Rules\n\nYou **MUST** read\n[references/firestore-rules-and-blueprint.md](references/firestore-rules-and-blueprint.md)\n(`/skills/system_skills/firebase-integration-rpc/references/firestore-rules-and-blueprint.md`)\nusing `view_file` and follow its complete workflow to author and lint\n`firestore.rules`:\n\n1.  Install `@firebase/eslint-plugin-security-rules`\n    (`npm install --save-dev @firebase/eslint-plugin-security-rules`) and\n    configure `firebaseRulesPlugin.configs[\'flat/recommended\']` in your ESLint\n    config (ignoring `dist/**/*`).\n2.  Draft rules in `DRAFT_firestore.rules` enforcing **The Eight Pillars of\n    Hardened Rules** (Master Gate, Validation Blueprints, Path Variable\n    Hardening, Tiered Identity Logic, Total Array Guarding, PII Isolation,\n    Atomicity Guarantee, and Secure List Queries) and complete Phases 0\342\200\2236\n    (Payload-First Security TDD in `security_spec.md` /\n    `firestore.rules.test.ts`, Codebase Analysis, Relationship Mapping,\n    Primitive Definition, Fortress Rules, Red Team Audit, and Final Production\n    Rules in `firestore.rules`).\n3.  Re-run ESLint against the rules after every update until zero errors or\n    vulnerabilities remain, finalize `firestore.rules`, and deploy rules via\n    `rpc_action` (`service_name: \"fax\"`, `method_name: \"DeployRules\"`).\n\n## 3. Create error handlers\n\nYou **MUST** read\n[references/error-handling-and-sdk.md](references/error-handling-and-sdk.md)\n(`/skills/system_skills/firebase-integration-rpc/references/error-handling-and-sdk.md`)\nusing `view_file` and implement the structured `OperationType`,\n`FirestoreErrorInfo`, and `handleFirestoreError(error, operationType, path)`\nhelper in every `try/catch` block and `onSnapshot` error callback so permission\ndenials surface rich context (`authInfo`, `operationType`, `path`) for debugging\nand wrapping in a React `ErrorBoundary`.\n\n## Firestore Basics\n\n*   **Documents:** Basic storage units (1MB limit). Do NOT store images/videos\n    directly.\n*   **Collections:** Containers for documents only.\n\n## Authentication\n\nOnly Google Login is configured by the ProvisionFirebase RPC, do not set up\nemail/password, anonymous auth or any other provider unless the user\nspecifically requests it. If they do you *MUST* walk them through the process of\nenabling those providers from the Firebase console.\n\nPrefer using `signInWithPopup` because this environment will allowlist the\njavascript URL but does not automatically update the redirect URLs needed for\n`signInWithRedirect`. If the user asks for `signInWithRedirect`, point them\ntowards the\n[best practices for signInWithRedirect](https://firebase.google.com/docs/auth/web/redirect-best-practices)\nand provide them the correct redirect url of the iframe.\n\n## Importing the Firebase SDK\n\nLoad configuration from `firebase-applet-config.json`.\n\n```ts\nimport { initializeApp } from \'firebase/app\';\nimport { getAuth } from \'firebase/auth\';\nimport { getFirestore } from \'firebase/firestore\';\nimport firebaseConfig from \'./firebase-applet-config.json\';\n\nconst app = initializeApp(firebaseConfig);\nexport const db = getFirestore(app, firebaseConfig.firestoreDatabaseId); /* CRITICAL: The app will break without this line */\nexport const auth = getAuth();\n```\n\n## React Firebase Setup\n\n*   **FirebaseProvider:** Initialize services, set up `onAuthStateChanged`, and\n    track readiness state.\n*   **Data Fetching:** Only attach `onSnapshot` listeners if auth is ready and\n    user is authenticated.\n*   **Defensive Payload Constraints:** Ensure that your generated application\n    components and API integration layers implement sufficient data\n    sanitization, input validation, or payload truncation logic. Outgoing\n    database mutations MUST be programmatically guaranteed to adhere to the\n    volumetric boundaries and structural constraints declared in the\n    `firebase-blueprint.json` schema prior to execution.\n*   **CRITICAL (Validation Synchronicity):** When designing `firestore.rules`\n    and defining the properties for your TypeScript components, you MUST parse\n    the entities declared in your `firebase-blueprint.json` file. Verbatim\n    inject the `maxLength` constants and `pattern` regex strings defined in the\n    blueprint directly into your security rules boolean expressions and your\n    frontend input validation hooks.\n\n## Critical Constraints\n\n*   Never skip error handling.\n*   Always ensure secure rules.\n*   Redeploy rules after any change.\n*   Re-run ESLint after any change to the rules.\n*   If Quota is exceeded, stop and inform the user.\n\n## Manual Firebase Setup / Remixed Apps\n\n*   If user manually sets up Firebase: Do NOT write config or deploy rules\n    automatically.\n*   If a remixed app has placeholder data: Prompt the user to configure\n    Firebase.\n\n## Firestore Limits\n\nThere are two editions of Firestore: **Standard** and **Enterprise**. The\n`ProvisionFirebase` RPC will create an app using Firestore **Enterprise**\nEdition. If the user is providing Firestore themselves, they may have set up\neither Enterprise or Standard edition.\n\nIn AI Studio, Firestore comes with free tier limit:\n\n-   **Firestore Usage Quota**: When the user hits a quota limit, they will\n    receive a runtime error in their application with message: \"Quota\n    exceeded.\". Inform the user that quota will reset the next day, and detailed\n    quota information can be found under the **Spark** plan column in the\n    **Enterprise edition** section of\n    https://firebase.google.com/pricing#cloud-firestore.\n-   **Specific Quota Error**: If the user encounters an error containing `\"Quota\n    exceeded for quota metric \'Free daily read units per project (free tier\n    database)\'\"`, you should provide them with a link to their database in the\n    format:\n    `https://console.firebase.google.com/project/<project-id>/firestore/databases/<firestore-database-id>/data?openUpgradeDialog=true`\n-   **Firestore Database Instances Quota**: When a user exceeds their\n    per-project database limit, they will receive an error from the\n    ProvisionFirebase RPC with the message, \"You have reached the maximum number\n    of databases per project.\". When this happens, stop generation, inform the\n    user about the error, and ask the user how they would like to proceed.\n    Suggest that they choose a different Firebase project, or delete any unused\n    databases in the Firebase console.\n\n## Final steps\n\nIt is **CRITICAL** that you finalize your implementation with the following\nsteps:\n\n1.  Analyze the user\'s application and note down all the collections and their\n    fields.\n2.  Test whether the user\'s application works by comparing the expected behavior\n    with the actual rules.\n3.  If the rules deny any operation that the application would allow, modify the\n    rules to allow the operation.\n4.  Re-run the \"Red Team\" Audit, and ensure that the rules are still secure.\n5.  If the rules are still not secure, go back to \"2. Generate Firestore Rules\"\n    and repeat the process.\n6.  If the rules are secure, generate the final `firestore.rules` file.\n7.  When the above steps are complete, **always encourage users to review** and\n    harden the generated rules before sharing an app broadly and **be humble**\n    when describing the quality and security of the rule set.\n\n## Limitations\n\n*   No direct DB manipulation.\n*   No server-side Firestore access or Auth user management through the Firebase\n    Admin SDK on Google-hosted (Starter Tier) projects. See\n    [Firebase Admin SDK (Server-Side Access)](#firebase-admin-sdk-server-side-access).\n"
