    thinking: "**Unveiling Design Flaws**\n\nI\'ve just reviewed `Museum3DView.tsx`, `museumService.ts`, `App.tsx`, and `firestore.rules`, and suddenly, the entire architecture clicks! The bugs and enhancements are now obvious. Specifically, I\'m focusing on the camera rotation issues within `Museum3DView.tsx` when the 3D model pivots. This initial area of interest is ripe for immediate improvement.\n\n\n**Addressing Camera Inconsistencies**\n\nThe code in `Museum3DView.tsx` exposes major camera/avatar rotation inconsistencies.  \'A\' and \'D\' keys strafe without rotating the camera, and arrow keys only rotate the camera, leaving the avatar frozen! The user\'s feedback reinforces these issues. Now I\'m focused on synchronizing these rotations for a seamless experience.\n\n\n**Synchronizing Controls & Camera**\n\nI\'m now implementing the perfect museum camera and steering controls. Pressing \'A\' or \'D\' should rotate both the character and the camera smoothly, and I\'m adding `Q`, `E`, and virtual keys to be supported as well. I\'m building this as a unit-tested helper file to ensure the math is decoupled. I\'m paying very close attention to making sure the yaw conventions are correct.\n\n\n**Verifying Yaw Conventions**\n\nI\'ve been meticulously tracing the yaw calculations. I\'ve re-examined how `camYaw` influences the forward vector and avatar direction. It confirms my initial understanding: the `Math.atan2` function correctly derives the avatar\'s facing direction. I\'m verifying specific angles like 0 and \302\261\317\200/2 to ensure precise alignment. Now, I\'m verifying the camera position calculations based on the yaw to make sure it tracks and orbits the avatar correctly, particularly with varying distances.\n\n\n**Refining Camera Angles**\n\nI\'m now deeply immersed in the camera\'s position relative to the avatar. Right now, I\'m testing the calculations for the camera\'s offset from the avatar\'s position based on `camYaw`. With the camera looking directly over the character\'s back, I\'m thinking about walking while turning. Specifically, I\'m ensuring smooth rotation and movement, accounting for combinations of forward/backward motion with the turning controls. I\'m focusing on user control.\n\n\n**Integrating Mouse Drags**\n\nI\'m now integrating mouse drags for camera control, ensuring they orbit `camYaw` and `camPitch` as previously implemented. Crucially, pressing \'W\' after a drag should smoothly walk the avatar in the camera\'s new direction. I\'ve also identified a crucial bug on line 685 in `Museum3DView.tsx`: it didn\'t wrap `angleDiff`, so remote users had crazy 360\302\260 spins. I\'ll normalize with `normalizeAngleDiff`. Furthermore, I\'ve noticed how \'yaw\' clamping might affect `firestore.rules`, and I will normalize `yaw` before broadcasting.\n\n\n**Tracing Email Authorizations**\n\nI\'m now tracing the email authorizations, focusing on the scenarios where `abidsarker213@gmail.com`, `abidsarker098@gmail.com`, and `suraia475@gmail.com` interact in Firestore. I am examining `museumService.ts` and `firestore.rules` for email access issues. I\'m prioritizing user flow for existing museums. I\'m ensuring all pre-authorized emails are added to member lists and permissions.\n\n\n**Implementing Initial Authorization**\n\nOkay, the museum ID is confirmed as `museum_CwufCmYF1kOQRybSTFLoktJuo8b2`, owned by `abidsarker213@gmail.com`. I\'m now crafting the logic for `ensurePersonalMuseum` to synchronize the default trusted circle (`abidsarker098@gmail.com`, `suraia475@gmail.com`) upon `abidsarker213@gmail.com`\'s sign-in. This includes updating `museum.memberEmails`, deterministic member document IDs, and syncing viewer lists in existing artworks. I\'m scrutinizing `firestore.rules` to address the scenario where `suraia475@gmail.com` or `abidsarker098@gmail.com` might sign in *before* Abid, ensuring they have appropriate access.\n\n\n**Analyzing Authorization Flow**\n\nI\'m now focusing on ensuring that pre-authorized users like `abidsarker098@gmail.com` and `suraia475@gmail.com` gain access even if `abidsarker213@gmail.com` (the owner) hasn\'t opened the app post-update. I\'m examining the `firestore.rules`, specifically lines 264\342\200\223267, 303\342\200\223307, and 396\342\200\223401, to understand how `memberEmails` and queries interact. I\'m checking the query filters to ensure access when the `memberEmails` array is still empty.\n\n\n**Evaluating Trusted Circle Safety**\n\nI\'m now diving into the implications of hardcoding a \"trusted circle\" rule within `firestore.rules`. I need to ensure this approach aligns with the Eight Pillars of security and the `firebase-integration-rpc` skill. I\'ll thoroughly review the guidance in `/skills/system_skills/firebase-integration-rpc/references/firestore-rules-and-blueprint.md` before making any changes. This is important to ensure data integrity and user authorization.\n\n\n**Refining Model Presence**\n\nI\'m now implementing the 3D model presence logic. The product spec is clear: fake patrols are out! I\'m removing the sine wave \"walking\" in `Museum3DView.tsx`. When a user is online, their model should be active, but otherwise static. This aligns with the spec: \"tara jokhn mail dye dhukbe tokhn sei 3d model active hbe ar nahle soja ekjaygay daray thakbe.\" I\'m reviewing `App.tsx` and `Museum3DView.tsx` to align the code.\n\n\n**Implementing User Presence**\n\nI\'m now implementing real-time user presence. Every movement is synced via Firestore, so both Abid and Suraia see each other smoothly. Each 3D model now has their nameplate and an active accent. If a user is offline, their last position and avatar are preserved. Furthermore, when a trusted member hasn\'t visited yet, the system retains their data.\n\n\n**Clarifying Model Visibility**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the logic for model visibility in offline scenarios. My current task is making sure that a user who is offline is represented correctly. When offline, I\'m ensuring their model appears static, retaining their last position. This means the 3D models should stand straight and still. This ensures the correct behavior of the models. I\'m focusing on ensuring models for \'trusted members\' are present.\n\n\n**Refining Member Subscriptions**\n\nI\'m now revising the member subscription logic within `Museum3DView.tsx`. The code on line 133, specifically the case-insensitive `displayName` check, requires reevaluation, to ensure it doesn\'t break. I\'m focusing on ensuring all members are appropriately included, regardless of capitalization, especially for those in the trusted circle, like `suraia475@gmail.com`.\n\n\n**Resolving Member Presence Duplication**\n\nI\'ve discovered that matching live presence to invited members was previously done using `displayName`, which can cause duplicates. To fix this, I\'m now storing the lowercase `email` on each `presence` document to ensure each user (`abidsarker213@gmail.com`, `abidsarker098@gmail.com`, `suraia475@gmail.com`, etc.) has exactly one 3D model. I\'m also addressing a related bug in `subscribeMembers` (`museumService.ts` line 436) and `firestore.rules` (line 361) that was preventing pre-authorized users from seeing other members in the museum. The default museum selection for pre-authorized users is being addressed.\n\n\n"
