    content: "---\nname: threejs\ndescription: >-\n  Comprehensive guide for Three.js development. Use when creating 3D scenes,\n  animating objects, handling user interaction, loading assets, applying\n  materials and textures, or creating custom shaders and post-processing\n  effects.\n---\n\n# Three.js Skill\n\nThis skill provides a comprehensive reference for Three.js development, split\ninto specialized topics.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT] **MANDATORY REFERENCE LOADING**\n>\n> Before writing any Three.js or WebGPU/TSL code, you **MUST** identify the\n> relevant topic(s) below and read the corresponding reference file(s) (e.g.\n> `/skills/system_skills/threejs/references/fundamentals.md`). Do NOT rely on\n> generic assumptions without reading the topic references first.\n\n## Topics\n\n-   **[Fundamentals](references/fundamentals.md)**: Scene setup, cameras,\n    renderer, Object3D hierarchy, coordinate systems.\n-   **[Animation](references/animation.md)**: Keyframe animation, skeletal\n    animation, morph targets, animation mixing.\n-   **[Geometry](references/geometry.md)**: Built-in shapes, BufferGeometry,\n    custom geometry, instancing.\n-   **[Interaction](references/interaction.md)**: Raycasting, controls,\n    mouse/touch input, object selection.\n-   **[Lighting](references/lighting.md)**: Light types, shadows, environment\n    lighting.\n-   **[Loaders](references/loaders.md)**: Asset loading - GLTF, textures,\n    images, models, async patterns.\n-   **[Materials](references/materials.md)**: PBR, basic, phong, shader\n    materials, material properties.\n-   **[Post-Processing](references/postprocessing.md)**: EffectComposer, bloom,\n    DOF, screen effects.\n-   **[Shaders](references/shaders.md)**: GLSL, ShaderMaterial, uniforms, custom\n    effects.\n-   **[Textures](references/textures.md)**: Texture types, UV mapping,\n    environment maps, texture settings.\n\n## WebGPU & TSL Topics\n\n-   **[WebGPU Reference](references/webgpu-reference.md)**: TSL syntax, types,\n    operators, and quick reference.\n-   **[WebGPU Core Concepts](references/webgpu-core-concepts.md)**: Variables,\n    control flow, functions, and time.\n-   **[WebGPU Materials](references/webgpu-materials.md)**: Node materials, PBR\n    properties, and vertex manipulation.\n-   **[WebGPU Compute Shaders](references/webgpu-compute-shaders.md)**: Storage\n    buffers, workgroups, and atomic operations.\n-   **[WebGPU Post-Processing](references/webgpu-post-processing.md)**:\n    RenderPipeline, built-in effects, and custom effects.\n-   **[WGSL Integration](references/webgpu-wgsl-integration.md)**: Embedding raw\n    WGSL code in TSL.\n-   **[WebGPU Device Loss](references/webgpu-device-loss.md)**: Handling GPU\n    device loss and recovery.\n-   **[WebGPU Limits & Features](references/webgpu-limits-and-features.md)**:\n    Querying and requesting GPU limits.\n-   **[WebGPU Examples](references/webgpu-examples.md)**: Complete examples of\n    WebGPU scenes.\n-   **[WebGPU Templates](references/webgpu-templates.md)**: Starter templates\n    for projects and compute shaders.\n\n## General Best Practices\n\n1.  **Resource Management**: Always dispose of geometries, materials, and\n    textures when they are no longer needed to prevent memory leaks.\n2.  **Performance**: Limit draw calls by merging geometries or using\n    `InstancedMesh`. Use appropriate texture sizes and segment counts.\n3.  **Color Space**: Ensure correct color space settings (usually\n    `SRGBColorSpace` for color textures and output).\n4.  **3D Game Development**: When building interactive 3D games (with player\n    controls, physics, collision detection, scoring, health, and game state\n    transitions), combine responsive keyboard/mouse/touch controls and game\n    state management with a `THREE.Clock` (`getDelta()`) game loop and the\n    Three.js studio PBR rendering and zero-404 asset directives below.\n\n## Three.js Architecture, Performance, Visual Realism & UX Directives\n\n> **Purpose**: These directives act as an additive quality, performance,\n> rendering, and UX enhancer on top of your natural domain reasoning and modular\n> application architecture.\n\n### 1. Respect Your Modular Architecture & Creative Depth\n\n-   **Preserve Modular Decomposition**: Whether you are building a multi-file\n    TypeScript/React application (`/src/...`) or a single-file HTML experience,\n    retain your full, deep domain decomposition (e.g., dedicated modules for\n    procedural geometry generation, material libraries, kinematics/physics,\n    lighting rigs, audio synthesis, and UI state).\n-   **Never Compromise Domain Richness**: Do not simplify or omit intricate\n    mechanical, mathematical, or visual details. Proceed with your full\n    hierarchical blueprint, realistic sub-components, and coordinated\n    mechanical/physical ratios.\n-   **Composite 3\342\200\22312 Part Procedural Models (`THREE.Group`)**: Never represent a\n    hero subject, vehicle, character, tree, or structure with a single default\n    primitive (`BoxGeometry`, `SphereGeometry`, `CylinderGeometry`,\n    `CapsuleGeometry`). Assemble procedural 3D subjects as a `THREE.Group` of\n    **3\342\200\22312 distinct parts** across three silhouette scales: **(1) Primary\n    silhouette** (hull, trunk, main body), **(2) Secondary read** (wheels,\n    canopy, turret, wings, roof), and **(3) Accent detail** (visor, headlights,\n    exhaust, trim), using `ExtrudeGeometry` (with bevels), `LatheGeometry`, or\n    `TubeGeometry` where appropriate. Vary `roughness` by `>= 0.25` (and\n    `metalness`) between adjacent parts, apply subtle per-instance scale\n    (`\302\26115%`) and `setColorAt` tint variation on `InstancedMesh` foliage or\n    rocks, and pivot every prop and actor at bottom-center so it sits flush on\n    the ground (`groundHeightAt(x, z)`) with nothing floating.\n-   **Natural Outdoor Perimeters vs. Indoor Walls**: Indoor scenes use walls,\n    but **outdoor arenas, villages, forests, and terrains must never be boxed\n    inside a rectangular indoor wall floating in an empty void**\342\200\224extend terrain\n    beyond the playable zone and bound outdoor perimeters with natural barriers\n    (cliffs, rock outcrops, dense tree groves, water/coast, fences) and horizon\n    fog fade-off.\n-   **Zero External File / 404 Dependencies**: All textures, surface finishes,\n    normal maps, gear teeth profiles, and audio must be procedurally generated\n    in code (HTML5 2D Canvas, `DataTexture`, mathematical `ExtrudeGeometry` /\n    `LatheGeometry` / `BufferGeometry`, and Web Audio API oscillators). Never\n    reference unhosted local `.png`, `.glb`, `.json`, or `.mp3` files.\n\n### 2. Rendering, PBR Materials & Texture Fidelity\n\nWhen configuring your Three.js renderer, materials (`materials.ts`), and\nprocedural textures, enforce these exact physical rendering standards:\n\n1.  **Modern Renderer, Software-WebGL Guard & Color Pipeline (Three.js r160+)**:\n\n    Detect software WebGL (SwiftShader, llvmpipe, WARP) from the GPU renderer\n    string, never the user agent. This covers the GPU-less headless browser and\n    real users on VDI or with hardware acceleration off. Use `isSoftwareGl` only\n    for rendering cost (pixel ratio, antialiasing, shadows, multi-pass effects,\n    frame pacing). Never change what is in the scene (objects, counts, geometry\n    detail), the layout, the UI, or app logic, so the screenshots you verify\n    show the same app GPU users get.\n\n    ```ts\n    const isSoftwareGl = (() => {\n      const gl = document.createElement(\'canvas\').getContext(\'webgl2\');\n      if (!gl) return true;\n      const ext = gl.getExtension(\'WEBGL_debug_renderer_info\');\n      const param = ext ? ext.UNMASKED_RENDERER_WEBGL : gl.RENDERER;\n      const gpu = String(gl.getParameter(param));\n      gl.getExtension(\'WEBGL_lose_context\')?.loseContext(); // free the probe\n      return /SwiftShader|llvmpipe|softpipe|Software|Basic Render/i.test(gpu);\n    })();\n    const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({\n      antialias: !isSoftwareGl,\n      powerPreference: \'high-performance\',\n    });\n    renderer.outputColorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace;\n    renderer.toneMapping = THREE.ACESFilmicToneMapping;\n    renderer.toneMappingExposure = 1.1;\n    // Cap at 1.0 on software WebGL, or 2.0 on desktop/mobile GPUs.\n    renderer.setPixelRatio(\n      isSoftwareGl ? 1 : Math.min(window.devicePixelRatio, 2),\n    );\n    renderer.shadowMap.enabled = !isSoftwareGl;\n    renderer.shadowMap.type = THREE.PCFSoftShadowMap;\n    ```\n\n2.  **Mandatory Studio Environment Reflection (`RoomEnvironment`)**:\n\n    -   High-metalness materials (`metalness > 0.5`: brushed gold, platinum,\n        blued steel, chrome) and transmissive glass (`transmission > 0.8`:\n        sapphire crystal, optical lenses) appear dark or flat without an\n        environment map.\n    -   Always generate a zero-dependency procedural studio environment map\n        during scene initialization, on every device. When `isSoftwareGl`, keep\n        it and the same light intensities so verified screenshots match what GPU\n        users see, and skip only multi-pass / per-fragment software bottlenecks\n        (post-processing, `RectAreaLight`, and `transmission`):\n\n        ```ts\n        import { RoomEnvironment } from\n          \'three/addons/environments/RoomEnvironment.js\';\n\n        const pmremGenerator = new THREE.PMREMGenerator(renderer);\n        scene.environment = pmremGenerator.fromScene(\n          new RoomEnvironment(),\n          0.04,\n        ).texture;\n        pmremGenerator.dispose();\n        ```\n\n3.  **Render on Demand; Pace Loops on Software WebGL**:\n\n    -   **On-demand scenes** (static between interactions): on every device,\n        coalesce mount, state/prop updates, `OrbitControls` `\'change\'`,\n        `resize`, and active transitions through the `requestRender()` below (it\n        draws on the next frame and, when `isSoftwareGl`, holds further draws\n        until `afterDraw`) \342\200\224 never call `renderer.render()` synchronously inside\n        event handlers or run an unpaced loop. The one exception is exporting\n        the canvas: call `renderer.render()` right before `toDataURL()` /\n        `toBlob()` in the same handler, since the drawing buffer is cleared\n        after each frame is shown. When `isSoftwareGl`, disable `enableDamping`\n        and `autoRotate`.\n    -   **Continuous loops** (games, physics, particle/wave simulations): loop\n        with `requestAnimationFrame` on GPUs. When `isSoftwareGl`, always\n        schedule the next frame with `afterDraw(tick)`, which waits for the CPU\n        draw to finish and then pauses 80 ms. Never call `setTimeout(tick, n)`\n        right after `render()` (it returns before drawing finishes) or throttle\n        rAF by timestamp; both still starve the main thread and page rendering:\n\n        ```ts\n        let frameId = 0;\n        function afterDraw(cb: () => void) {\n          const gl = renderer.getContext() as WebGL2RenderingContext;\n          const sync = gl.fenceSync(gl.SYNC_GPU_COMMANDS_COMPLETE, 0);\n          gl.flush();\n          const poll = () => {\n            const state = sync && gl.getSyncParameter(sync, gl.SYNC_STATUS);\n            if (state === gl.UNSIGNALED) {\n              frameId = window.setTimeout(poll, 5);\n            } else {\n              gl.deleteSync(sync);\n              frameId = window.setTimeout(cb, 80);\n            }\n          };\n          frameId = window.setTimeout(poll, 5);\n        }\n        function tick() {\n          // ...advance the scene by elapsed time, not a fixed amount per frame...\n          renderer.render(scene, camera);\n          if (isSoftwareGl) afterDraw(tick); // never setTimeout(tick, n)\n          else frameId = requestAnimationFrame(tick);\n        }\n        // On-demand scenes: call requestRender() instead of looping tick().\n        let renderBusy = false;\n        let renderDirty = false;\n        function requestRender() {\n          if (renderBusy) {\n            renderDirty = true;\n            return;\n          }\n          renderBusy = true;\n          frameId = requestAnimationFrame(() => {\n            renderDirty = false;\n            controls.update(); // ...advance transitions...\n            renderer.render(scene, camera);\n            const done = () => {\n              renderBusy = false;\n              if (renderDirty) requestRender();\n            };\n            if (isSoftwareGl) afterDraw(done);\n            else done();\n          });\n        }\n        ```\n\n4.  **Advanced PBR Material & Shader Extension (`MeshPhysicalMaterial` &\n    `onBeforeCompile`)**:\n\n    -   **Anisotropic Brushed Metals**: For luxury brushed gold, titanium, or\n        steel, enable `anisotropy: 0.8` and `anisotropyRotation` alongside\n        `metalness: 0.95` and `roughness: 0.15`.\n    -   **Optical Glass & Crystals**: Use `transmission: isSoftwareGl ? 0 :\n        0.95` (with `transparent: true, opacity: isSoftwareGl ? 0.35 : 1`\n        fallback since `transmission` triggers an extra scene render pass),\n        `ior: 1.55 - 2.4`, `thickness: 1.5`, `attenuationColor`, and `clearcoat:\n        1.0`.\n    -   **Extending Built-in PBR Materials (`onBeforeCompile`)**: When adding\n        procedural waves, ripples, or vertex displacement to an object that\n        still needs full PBR lighting, shadows, and environment reflections,\n        extend `MeshStandardMaterial` / `MeshPhysicalMaterial` via\n        `material.onBeforeCompile` (injecting custom GLSL at `#include\n        <begin_vertex>` or `#include <color_fragment>`) rather than falling back\n        to unlit `ShaderMaterial`.\n\n5.  **Procedural Texture Color Space & Anisotropy**:\n\n    -   **Color Space**: For procedurally generated color/albedo maps\n        (`CanvasTexture` / `DataTexture`), explicitly set `texture.colorSpace =\n        THREE.SRGBColorSpace`. Leave data maps (normal, roughness, metalness) in\n        linear space (`NoColorSpace`).\n    -   **Grazing-Angle Sharpness**: Set `texture.anisotropy =\n        renderer.capabilities.getMaxAnisotropy();` on all procedural dials,\n        grids, and fine markings so they stay razor-sharp when viewed at shallow\n        camera angles.\n    -   **Tangents for Normal Maps**: When applying normal maps or anisotropy to\n        custom procedural geometries (`ExtrudeGeometry`, `LatheGeometry`), call\n        `geometry.computeTangents()`.\n\n6.  **Multi-Layer Glass, Decals & Transparency Sorting**:\n\n    -   **Glass Vessels & Domes**: Set `depthWrite: false` and explicit\n        `renderOrder` on outer glass layers so interior mechanisms remain crisp\n        and never flicker.\n    -   **Coplanar Z-Fighting**: When placing floor grids, chapter rings, or\n        decals directly on another surface, set `material.polygonOffset = true;\n        material.polygonOffsetFactor = -1; material.polygonOffsetUnits = -1;`.\n\n### 3. Studio Lighting, Area Softboxes & Contact Grounding\n\n1.  **Balanced Studio Rig (\342\211\244 3 Shadow Casters)**:\n    -   Configure a 3-point lighting rig (Key Light with `castShadow = true`,\n        tight orthographic shadow bounds `left/right/top/bottom = \302\261d`,\n        `shadow.bias = -0.0005`, `shadow.normalBias = 0.02`; Fill Light;\n        Rim/Backlight).\n    -   **Warm/Cool Color-Temperature Contrast & Horizon Fog**: Pair a warm\n        directional key light against a cool sky/ground `THREE.HemisphereLight`\n        fill (never rely on flat white `AmbientLight` alone), and match\n        `scene.fog` (`Fog` or `FogExp2`) to the horizon/sky background color so\n        distant geometry blends seamlessly.\n    -   **Studio Softboxes (`RectAreaLight`)**: For luxury product showcases\n        (watches, cars, jewelry), initialize `RectAreaLightUniformsLib.init()`\n        and add `THREE.RectAreaLight` studio light panels for authentic soft,\n        elongated specular highlights across metallic and glass bevels (skip\n        them when `isSoftwareGl`).\n2.  **Contact Shadow Grounding**:\n    -   Prevent hero objects from looking like they are floating in empty space\n        by placing a soft radial contact shadow plane directly beneath the\n        object (`transparent: true, depthWrite: false, opacity: 0.45`) or using\n        `ContactShadows`, and offset stacked horizontal surfaces (floors, roads,\n        rugs, decals) by `>= 0.02` on Y so coplanar faces never Z-fight.\n\n### 4. Runtime Performance, Kinematics & Raycasting Discipline\n\n1.  **Stable WebGL Lifecycle in React (`useRef` Container)**:\n    -   When mounting inside React, place the WebGL `<canvas>` inside a\n        dedicated empty `<div ref={canvasMountRef} />` outside sibling React HUD\n        overlays. Initialize `WebGLRenderer` and the scene once (`useEffect(...,\n        [])`, calling `cancelAnimationFrame(frameId); clearTimeout(frameId);`\n        and `renderer.dispose()` on unmount), call `renderer.compile(scene,\n        camera)` once after setup to pre-warm shaders (skip it when\n        `isSoftwareGl`), and store callbacks/mutable state in `useRef` so React\n        UI state updates never tear down the canvas or reset camera/actor\n        positions.\n    -   Never apply CSS `backdrop-filter` / `backdrop-blur` on UI/HUD elements\n        over an active WebGL canvas (use solid or `rgba(...)` surfaces so\n        software WebGL rendering never stalls).\n2.  **Silky-Smooth Exponential Damping & Spring Kinematics**:\n    -   Never snap camera presets, exploded-view layer separations, or UI\n        transitions abruptly. Use frame-rate independent exponential damping\n        (`THREE.MathUtils.damp(current, target, lambda, delta)` or `1 -\n        Math.exp(-k * dt)`) or second-order spring physics (`stiffness`,\n        `damping`, `velocity`) inside the animation loop so movements glide with\n        physical weight and momentum. In on-demand scenes, keep calling\n        `requestRender()` each frame until active transitions settle, and cancel\n        scripted camera transitions on arrival or when user controls start\n        (`OrbitControls` `\'start\'`) so they never fight manual camera movement.\n3.  **Zero Garbage Collection (GC) in the Render Loop**:\n    -   Pre-allocate reusable math helpers (`const _vec3 = new THREE.Vector3()`,\n        `_mat4`, `_quat`, `_color`) at module scope outside the animation loop.\n        Never instantiate `new THREE.Vector3()` inside the per-frame tick.\n4.  **Instancing & Frustum Culling Safety**:\n    -   Whenever rendering > 20 identical repeated items (screws, gear teeth,\n        stars, trees, grid nodes), group them into a `THREE.InstancedMesh`.\n        Always call `instancedMesh.computeBoundingSphere()` after setting\n        instance matrices (or set `frustumCulled = false` if instances move\n        dynamically) so instances never vanish when rotating the camera.\n5.  **High-Performance Raycasting**:\n    -   When implementing interactive hover/click inspection on complex models,\n        restrict raycasting to a dedicated layer (`raycaster.layers.set(1)`) or\n        invisible simplified proxy collision boxes, and throttle `pointermove`\n        raycasts so pointer movement never causes frame drops.\n\n### 5. Ergonomic UI Layout: Collapsible Bottom Dock (Zero Viewport Occlusion)\n\nWhen building your interactive UI overlay (`ControlOverlay.tsx` / HTML HUD),\nkeep the central 3D viewport 100% unobstructed so the user can admire your 3D\nwork without UI panels covering the model:\n\n```\n\342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220\n\342\224\202  [ Minimal Title / Spec Badge (Top-Left or Top-Center) ]   \342\224\202\n\342\224\202                                                            \342\224\202\n\342\224\202                                                            \342\224\202\n\342\224\202                     3D SCENE VIEWPORT                      \342\224\202\n\342\224\202                (100% Unobstructed Center)                  \342\224\202\n\342\224\202                                                            \342\224\202\n\342\224\202                                                            \342\224\202\n\342\224\202               [ \342\226\276 Collapse / Expand HUD ]                  \342\224\202\n\342\224\202  \342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220  \342\224\202\n\342\224\202  \342\224\202 \342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\227\217\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220 Exploded Slider | Finishes | Camera | Audio  \342\224\202  \342\224\202\n\342\224\202  \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230  \342\224\202\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230\n```\n\n1.  **Unified Bottom Control Dock**:\n    -   Consolidate **all** interactive controls (exploded-view slider, material\n        finish swatches, lighting presets, camera view pills, time/speed\n        multipliers) into **one sleek, tabbed or compact horizontal glassmorphic\n        dock pinned to the bottom center** (`bottom: 20px; left: 50%; transform:\n        translateX(-50%); max-width: min(940px, 94vw);`).\n    -   **Never place floating control cards on the mid-left, mid-right, or\n        top-right flanks** where they collide with and cover the 3D subject.\n2.  **Collapsible \"Cinema / Zen Mode\" Drawer**:\n    -   Include a clean collapse/expand toggle button (`\342\226\276 Hide Controls` / `\342\226\264\n        Show Controls` or keyboard shortcut `H`) attached to the top edge of the\n        bottom dock.\n    -   When collapsed, smoothly slide the dock downward off-screen so the user\n        can inspect the 3D scene in full-screen glory or capture clean\n        screenshots.\n3.  **Clean, Static Status Indicators (No Pulsating Dots)**:\n    -   **Never use animated pulsating or glowing dots (`@keyframes pulse`)** in\n        status bars, badges, or buttons. Use clean, static typography, icons, or\n        subtle color swatches so the UI never distracts the eye from the 3D\n        motion.\n\n### 6. Quick Self-Audit Before Finalizing Code\n\n-   [ ] **Composite 3\342\200\22312 Part Procedural Models**: Are hero subjects, vehicles,\n    trees, and structures built as 3\342\200\22312 part `THREE.Group` meshes (with varied\n    roughness/metalness and grounded bottom-center pivots) rather than single\n    default primitives?\n-   [ ] **Environment Map & Warm/Cool Lighting**: Is `scene.environment`\n    populated via `RoomEnvironment` + `PMREMGenerator` on every device (same\n    light intensities when `isSoftwareGl`), paired with a warm key light, cool\n    `HemisphereLight` fill, and horizon-matched `Fog` / `FogExp2`?\n-   [ ] **Software WebGL**: Is `isSoftwareGl` read from the renderer string and\n    used only for rendering cost (same scene content, layout, and logic), static\n    scenes rendered on demand via a coalesced `requestRender()`, and every\n    software-WebGL loop scheduled with `afterDraw(tick)`, never\n    `setTimeout(tick, n)` after `render()`?\n-   [ ] **Texture Sharpness & Color Space**: Are procedural color textures set\n    to `SRGBColorSpace` with `anisotropy =\n    renderer.capabilities.getMaxAnisotropy()`?\n-   [ ] **Stable Canvas & No Side-Panel Clutter**: Is the WebGL `<canvas>`\n    mounted in an isolated ref container, with all interactive controls\n    consolidated into a **collapsible bottom dock** (no `backdrop-filter` over\n    WebGL and zero left/right side cards covering the 3D object)?\n-   [ ] **No Pulsating Dots**: Are all UI indicators static and free of\n    distracting `@keyframes pulse` dots?\n-   [ ] **Smooth Damping**: Are exploded-view separations and camera preset\n    transitions smoothly interpolated using `THREE.MathUtils.damp(..., delta)`\n    or spring physics, and cleared on arrival or user control start?\n-   [ ] **Zero 404s**: Are all textures, materials, gear/component geometries,\n    and Web Audio sound effects 100% procedural?\n"
